# Introduction # {#introduction}

This paper proposes function parameter packs whose parameters share one declared element type and use ordinary parameter initialization. One declaration can replace families of fixed-arity overloads while retaining ordinary conversions to the declared type, and a separately deduced pack count usable in result types.

C++26 clause references use N5050. [[N5050]]

<table class="data example-comparison" id=pack-matrix>
<caption>Parameter-pack declarations in C++26. Each declaration is independent. The lower-right declaration is ill-formed today and would be permitted by this proposal.</caption>
<colgroup><col style="width: 24%"><col style="width: 38%"><col style="width: 38%"></colgroup>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Element types</th><th scope=col>Template parameter pack</th><th scope=col>Function parameter pack</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope=row>Heterogeneous</th>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
template <auto... vs>
void f();
</xmp>
</td>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
void f(auto... vs);
</xmp>
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope=row>Homogeneous</th>
<td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Legal since C++11: T is not a parameter pack, but vs is.
template <class T, T... vs>
void foo();
</xmp>
</td>
<td class=code-invalid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Proposed (ill-formed in C++26): vs is a pack of T parameters.
template <class T>
void foo(T... vs);
</xmp>
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The matrix shows the gap: C++26 permits three of the four forms. The other forms suggest that both template and function parameter packs can have either homogeneous or heterogeneous elements, so the missing lower-right form can be confusing. In current C++, <code>T... vs</code> declares a function parameter pack whose pattern expands an existing type parameter pack. In the lower-right declaration, <code>T</code> is one type parameter rather than a pack to expand. That form is ill-formed. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.spec.auto.general#2">[dcl.spec.auto.general]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct#25">[dcl.fct]/25</a> The proposal instead uses that single <code>T</code> as the type of each parameter while declaring <code>vs</code> as a function parameter pack.

Each element of the proposed function pack would be an ordinary function parameter initialized at the call. When its type is fixed or supplied explicitly, arguments with different source types can undergo ordinary conversions to that type. If the type is deduced, the arguments must satisfy the applicable deduction rules. The pack's length would be deduced at compile time and could determine a result type, while its values could be computed at runtime. The homogeneous template pack instead contains constant template arguments. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a>

In Abseil's <a href="#example-count"><code>Partial</code></a>, the pack count determines which offsets its result type permits querying. Abseil's authors want a warning for a signed variable but not a direct positive literal. Fixed <code>size_t</code> parameters expose the original expressions at the call, where diagnostics can distinguish them. Warnings still depend on the implementation and enabled options. A <code>std::convertible_to&lt;size_t&gt;</code> constraint on a deduced pack leaves conversions inside the forwarding function instead. Generated fixed-arity overloads can provide the conversions and count-dependent result types today and the proposal expresses them directly.

For CasADi's <a href="#example-casadi">matrix concatenation</a>, fixed parameters select the symbolic-matrix overload in both constructed operand orders, while a constrained deduced pack makes both calls ambiguous. The proposed homogeneous pack keeps the fixed destinations and positional calls in one declaration per operation without a fixed upper operand count. Current C++ can still accept arbitrary counts through an explicit-destination grouped call or preserve positional calls with fixed-arity wrappers.

The proposal permits one homogeneous pack at the end of a function's parameter list. Its declaration makes the function an abbreviated function template. Each specialization has an ordinary function type with a fixed number of parameters and is distinguished in part by the pack length. Existing pack expansions retain their meanings.

Explicit lengths and multiple homogeneous packs are out of scope. The named form <code>int... values</code> is unambiguous and can proceed independently of the <a href="#compatibility">compatibility decision</a> for unnamed packs. Those would reuse <code>int...</code>, which is deprecated but still means C varargs in C++26. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a>

# Motivating use cases # {#examples}

The examples concern two requirements that constraints on deduced argument types do not preserve: conversion diagnostics at the call and conversion ranking between overloaded interfaces. Abseil also needs the argument count in its result type. Current and proposed definitions are independent alternatives.

## Combine call-site diagnostics with count-dependent results ## {#example-count}

Abseil's internal <code>Layout&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> describes one array per element type in a single allocation. <code>Partial</code> takes element counts for the first arrays and records the number of arguments in its result type, <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>. The size values may be computed at runtime. [[ABSL-LAYOUT]]

For runtime <code>size_t</code> values <code>int_count</code> and <code>double_count</code>:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
using ArrayLayout =
    absl::container_internal::Layout<int, double, char>;

auto ints_known = ArrayLayout::Partial(int_count);
auto ints_and_doubles_known =
    ArrayLayout::Partial(int_count, double_count);
</xmp>

The first call returns <code>ArrayLayout::PartialType&lt;1&gt;</code> and permits querying the <code>int</code> and <code>double</code> array offsets. The second returns <code>ArrayLayout::PartialType&lt;2&gt;</code> and also permits querying the <code>char</code> array's offset:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto double_offset = ints_known.Offset<double>();
auto char_offset = ints_and_doubles_known.Offset<char>();
</xmp>

Calling <code>ints_known.Offset&lt;char&gt;()</code> is rejected at compile time because the preceding <code>double</code> array's size was not supplied. The argument count selects the result type and determines which offsets can be calculated. The runtime size values determine the offset values. A replacement for <code>Partial</code> needs to retain this contract. [[ABSL-LAYOUT]]

The shipped declaration deduces every argument type to obtain the count, then casts each argument to <code>size_t</code>. A homogeneous pack would deduce only the count and initialize <code>size_t</code> parameters at the call:

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>Abseil's <code>Partial</code> and a proposed homogeneous declaration. Both retain the argument-count check.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Abseil at d522178</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Sizes>
static constexpr PartialType<sizeof...(Sizes)>
Partial(Sizes&&... sizes) {
  static_assert(sizeof...(Sizes) +
      StaticSizeSeq::size() <= sizeof...(Ts));
  return PartialType<sizeof...(Sizes)>(
      static_cast<size_t>(
          std::forward<Sizes>(sizes))...);
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
static constexpr auto
Partial(size_t... sizes)
    -> PartialType<sizeof...(sizes)> {
  static_assert(sizeof...(sizes) +
      StaticSizeSeq::size() <= sizeof...(Ts));
  return PartialType<sizeof...(sizes)>(
      sizes...);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

For an <code>int signed_count</code>, compare <code>ArrayLayout::Partial(3)</code> with <code>ArrayLayout::Partial(signed_count)</code>. Abseil's authors want conversion warnings for the signed variable but not the positive literal. Their <a href="https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp/blob/d522178b54087d2c3855484c1899ce99ad278169/absl/container/internal/layout.h#L760-L780">comment</a> explains that explicit casts suppress warnings for both arguments. Implicit conversions inside the forwarding function's body can instead warn for both.

A <code>std::convertible_to&lt;size_t&gt;</code> constraint does not move those body conversions to the call. Fixed <code>size_t</code> parameters move the conversions to the call so the compiler sees the original argument expression (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/concept.convertible#1">[concept.convertible]/1</a> and <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#6">[expr.call]/6</a>).

Warnings depend on the implementation and enabled options. Negative integer arguments remain well-formed and convert to unsigned values. Ordinary parameter initialization also rejects explicit-only conversions accepted by the shipped <code>static_cast</code>, such as an explicit <code>operator size_t()</code> (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/conv.integral#3">[conv.integral]/3</a>, <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.conv#1">[over.match.conv]/1</a>, and <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.static.cast#5">[expr.static.cast]/5</a>).

Current C++ can provide the same parameter conversions and count-dependent result types through a bounded overload set. For plain <code>Layout&lt;Ts...&gt;</code> with no statically supplied sizes, generate one overload for each count from zero through <code>sizeof...(Ts)</code>. The header already expands an existing index pack through its <code>IntToSize</code> alias to give the <a href="https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp/blob/d522178b54087d2c3855484c1899ce99ad278169/absl/container/internal/layout.h#L399-L400">constructor</a> <code>size_t</code> parameters. In this fragment, <code>LayoutType&lt;N, Ts...&gt;</code> stands for Abseil's <code>PartialType&lt;N&gt;</code>:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <size_t>
using IntToSize = size_t;

template <class Seq, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverload;

template <size_t... Is, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverload<std::index_sequence<Is...>, Ts...> {
  static constexpr LayoutType<sizeof...(Is), Ts...>
  Partial(IntToSize<Is>... sizes) {
    return LayoutType<sizeof...(Is), Ts...>(sizes...);
  }
};

template <class Seq, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverloads;

template <size_t... Ns, class... Ts>
struct PartialOverloads<std::index_sequence<Ns...>, Ts...>
    : PartialOverload<std::make_index_sequence<Ns>, Ts...>... {
  using PartialOverload<std::make_index_sequence<Ns>, Ts...>::Partial...;
};
</xmp>

<code>Layout</code> then inherits <code>PartialOverloads&lt;std::make_index_sequence&lt;sizeof...(Ts) + 1&gt;, Ts...&gt;</code>. For <code>ArrayLayout</code>, <code>Ns...</code> enumerates the counts 0, 1, 2, and 3. Each count generates an index sequence <code>Is...</code> of that length. Expanding <code>IntToSize&lt;Is&gt;... sizes</code> gives an overload with one <code>size_t</code> parameter per index. The using-declaration combines the overloads and the call selects one with a fixed parameter count.

The homogeneous pack expresses that interface in one function declaration, without generating and combining overloads.

## Preserve conversion ranking without fixed-arity wrappers ## {#example-casadi}

CasADi builds symbolic matrix expressions. Its <code>horzcat</code>, <code>vertcat</code>, and <code>diagcat</code> functions concatenate matrices horizontally, vertically, and diagonally. At commit d30fbc4, each operation has positional overloads for two through six arguments, giving fifteen wrappers. Each parameter is a <code>const MatType&amp;</code>, with <code>MatType</code> fixed by the enclosing <code>SparsityInterface</code> specialization. [[CASADI-CONCAT]] Library code uses <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11/casadi/core/integration_tools.cpp#L930-L936"><code>vertcat(dx_cat, 1)</code></a>, relying on conversion of the scalar to the selected matrix type.

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>CasADi's vertical concatenation wrappers, with two arities shown, and a proposed replacement for the family. Both initialize references to a fixed matrix type.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>CasADi at d30fbc4</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
inline friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& x, const MatType& y) {
  return vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{x, y});
}

inline friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& x, const MatType& y,
    const MatType& z) {
  return vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{x, y, z});
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
friend MatType vertcat(
    const MatType& first,
    const MatType& second,
    const MatType&... rest) {
  return MatType::vertcat(
      std::vector<MatType>{first, second, rest...});
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

The ordinary parameters retain the minimum of two operands. The trailing pack removes the six-argument ceiling. The same change applies to horizontal and diagonal concatenation.

A constrained deduced pack also removes the overload family. Consider this alternative inside the same class:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Args>
  requires (sizeof...(Args) >= 2 &&
            (std::is_convertible_v<
                const Args&, const MatType&> && ...))
friend MatType vertcat(const Args&... args) {
  return MatType::vertcat(std::vector<MatType>{args...});
}
</xmp>

This alternative changes overload resolution. CasADi's <code>SX</code> is a matrix of symbolic expressions and <code>IM</code> is an integer matrix. For a mutable <code>SX symbolic</code> and <code>IM indices</code>, <a href="https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/d30fbc46a6cc7ca18b6e63f63d586069beaacd11/casadi/core/generic_matrix.hpp#L285-L288">indexing</a> produces a <code>SubIndex&lt;SX, IM&gt;</code> proxy derived from <code>SX</code>. [[CASADI-PROXY]] The following calls are constructed checks of that interface:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
vertcat(symbolic(indices), 1);
vertcat(1, symbolic(indices));
</xmp>

Argument-dependent lookup finds concatenation functions associated with both <code>SX</code> and the proxy's <code>IM</code> template argument. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/basic.lookup.argdep#3">[basic.lookup.argdep]/3</a> The existing fixed parameters select the <code>SX</code> overload in both calls: binding the proxy to its base class is better than converting it to <code>IM</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.ics.rank#2.1">[over.ics.rank]/2.1</a> With the deduced pack, both candidates instead bind the deduced argument types directly and satisfy the conversion constraints. Neither candidate is better, making both calls ambiguous. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2">[over.match.best.general]/2</a> The homogeneous declaration retains the fixed destination types used to rank these conversions.

Keeping only the first parameter as <code>const MatType&amp;</code> leaves the second call ambiguous. With two fixed parameters, <code>vertcat(1, 1, symbolic(indices))</code> is still ambiguous because the distinguishing operand is in the deduced tail.

Competing <code>std::initializer_list&lt;SX&gt;</code> and <code>std::initializer_list&lt;IM&gt;</code> overloads also make the grouped form ambiguous. List conversion ranking uses the worst element conversion. The scalar requires a user-defined conversion for either destination so the list ranking loses the distinction supplied by the indexed operand (<a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.ics.list#5">[over.ics.list]/5</a>).

Current C++ already supports an arbitrary operand count through an explicit destination, such as <code>SX::vertcat({symbolic(indices), 1})</code>. Keeping or generating fixed-arity wrappers also preserves the positional calls. A homogeneous pack expresses their parameter conversions directly, with one declaration per operation and no fixed upper bound on the operand count.

# Motivation and Scope # {#motivation}

Constrained packs, initializer lists, typed array references, and generated overload sets offer different combinations of ordinary conversion diagnostics, count-dependent result types, and ordinary conversion ranking.

## Requests for fixed parameter types ## {#expressivity}

<p>R2 cited eight Stack Overflow questions asking how to write a function with a homogeneous parameter pack. [[P1219R2]]</p>

<p>In May 2022, a programmer asked for a fixed parameter type with ordinary implicit conversions and explained why constraints on deduced types using <code>std::same_as</code> or <code>std::convertible_to</code> do not provide that behavior. [[SO-9]]
In January 2026, another asked how to declare a function taking an even number of <code>int</code> arguments and why templates were needed for concrete parameter types. [[SO-10]]</p>

## Initializer lists ## {#initializer-lists}

<table class=data>
<caption>Homogeneous function parameter packs compared with std::initializer_list.</caption>
    <tr><th></th><th><code>std::initializer_list</code></th><th>homogeneous packs</th></tr>
    <tr><th>Can the function be non-template?</th><td>Yes</td><td>No, even without a template head</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Can mutate elements?</th><td>The backing-array elements are const</td><td>Yes, when the declared element type permits it</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Can appear before other arguments?</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Get the number of elements</th><td><code class="prettyprint">x.size()</code></td><td><code class="prettyprint">sizeof...(x)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><th>Use the count in a return type or constraint?</th><td>No, the function parameter's value does not determine its function type or constraints</td><td>Yes, the count is fixed for each specialization</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Iterate over the elements</th><td>Range-based <code class="prettyprint">for</code></td><td>Fold expressions</td></tr>
</table>

<p><code>std::initializer_list::size()</code> is <code>constexpr</code>, so the size of a list usable in constant expressions can be computed at compile time. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/support.initlist">[support.initlist]</a> An ordinary initializer-list function parameter cannot supply that count for a return type or constraint, even when the caller passes a fixed number of elements. <code>sizeof...(x)</code> gives the specialization's pack length independently of parameter values, as required by Abseil's <code>PartialType</code> in [[#example-count]]. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#10">[temp.variadic]/10</a></p>

<p>A <code>std::initializer_list</code> provides access to an array of const elements. A list of <code>std::reference_wrapper&lt;T&gt;</code> can still permit modification of the referenced objects. Const list elements therefore do not by themselves prevent mutation of caller-owned objects. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/initializer.list.syn#1">[initializer.list.syn]/1</a></p>

An initializer list also permits ownership transfer through user-defined wrappers with <code>mutable</code> members. Those members can be moved from even though the list elements are <code>const</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.stc#9">[dcl.stc]/9</a> Initializer-list copies share these members. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/initializer.list.syn#1">[initializer.list.syn]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list#5">[dcl.init.list]/5</a> Consuming a member through one copy leaves its moved-from state visible through the others. The wrapper does not make the list's length part of the function type.

Competing initializer-list overloads also rank conversions differently from separate parameters. In CasADi's case, grouping the operands loses the distinction between the matrix conversions ([[#example-casadi]]). Constructor overload selection has a further difference, described in [[#constructors]].

<aside class=issue>
<p>Confirm with Jan and Eczbek that Abseil's <code>Layout::Partial</code> is the maintained interface to keep in [[#example-count]]. Compare homogeneous packs with ranges.</p>
</aside>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Compare typed runtime variadics and checked C varargs, including D's variadics and GCC's argument-count builtin. Distinguish count from type checks and account for default promotions. <a href="https://dlang.org/spec/function.html#typesafe_variadic_functions">D typesafe variadics</a> <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Constructing-Calls.html#index-_005f_005fbuiltin_005fva_005farg_005fpack_005flen">GCC <code>__builtin_va_arg_pack_len</code></a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#12">[expr.call]/12</a></p>
</aside>

# Design Decisions # {#design-decisions}

## Definitions ## {#definitions}

A <em>homogeneous parameter pack</em> (or <em>homogeneous pack</em>) contains values of one type and its declaration introduces an independent count. A <em>heterogeneous parameter pack</em> (or <em>heterogeneous pack</em>) permits values of different types. Both terms apply to function parameter packs and template parameter packs.

A function parameter pack's <em>parameter pattern</em> is its parameter declaration with the declarator ellipsis removed. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6.1">[temp.variadic]/6.1</a> For an <code>auto...</code> parameter, ordinary placeholder processing first invents a type parameter pack. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct#22">[dcl.fct]/22</a> After placeholder processing, a declaration is homogeneous only if its pattern contains no unexpanded pack. An expansion of an existing pack remains ordinary even when all resulting parameter types are the same.

## Pack declarations ## {#implicit-template}

The same declarator syntax can express either an ordinary expansion or a new homogeneous pack. The pattern need not mention a template parameter:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// heterogeneous function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
template <class T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
void f(int... v);
</xmp>

A parameter pack declared with <code>auto... v</code> already permits different deduced argument types in C++26. The fixed-type form <code>int... v</code> is currently ill-formed. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a>

A homogeneous function parameter pack makes the declaration an abbreviated function template. Its signature establishes that status before the body is parsed.

[[P1061R10]] section 3.3 removed support for structured binding packs outside templates after objections to R9's implicit template region. That region covered the rest of an ordinary function after a structured binding declaration. Here the signature declares the whole function a template. This distinction does not establish committee agreement on the proposal.

In a function call, the pack's size is deduced from the arguments. This revision limits a function template to one homogeneous pack at the end of its parameter list, with no following C ellipsis. Ordinary parameters may precede the pack, as in [[#example-casadi]]. Once its parameters are formed, the pack behaves like any other function parameter pack.

The following declarations violate those placement rules:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void misplaced(int... values, int last);
void two_packs(int... left, double... right);
void trailing_varargs(int... values, ...);
</xmp>

A homogeneous pack declares parameters of the function itself or a lambda's call operator. The declarations <code>using Callback = void(int... values);</code> and <code>void register_callback(void (*callback)(int... values));</code> remain ill-formed. Ordinary pack expansions in aliases and nested function types are unchanged.

[[P2347R2]] proposes deducing the length of a single non-trailing function parameter pack from the number of arguments and required trailing parameters. Extending homogeneous packs to non-trailing positions is outside this revision's scope.

### Abbreviated syntax ### {#abbreviated-syntax}

The February 2019 Kona EWGI poll in [[#straw-polls]] records support for R2's named form, not acceptance of this revision or its wording. [[P1219-EWG-RECORD]]

Since C++20, a function template that needs no template parameter names can use abbreviated syntax, with placeholders such as <code>auto</code> in its parameter list. [[P1141R2]] <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct#22">[dcl.fct]/22</a>

If the homogeneous pack is the only reason the declaration is a template and its pattern contains no dependent names, the template has no formal template parameters. The <code nohighlight>template-head</code> must then be omitted. An empty head would be unambiguous here, but the proposal disallows it on template declarations to avoid confusion with explicit specializations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
void foo(T... v); // #1 OK

// #2 OK, declares a template with an empty template parameter list
void foo(int... v);

// Error: function parameter pack in explicit specialization
template <>
void foo(float... v);

// OK, declares an explicit specialization of #2
template <>
void foo<>(int a, int b);

// OK, declares an explicit specialization of #1
template <>
void foo<int>(int a, int b);
</xmp>

### Type deduction ### {#deduction}

In these proposed declarations, <code>sum</code> fixes the element type as <code>int</code> while <code>total</code> uses the ordinary template parameter <code>T</code>.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum(int... values) {
    return (0 + ... + values);
}

template <class T>
T total(T... values) {
    return (T{} + ... + values);
}
</xmp>

For <code>total</code> without an explicit template argument, <code>T</code> is deduced from every applicable argument. This deduction neither chooses the first argument's type nor computes a common type. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call#1">[temp.deduct.call]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#2">[temp.deduct.type]/2</a> With <code>sum</code> or <code>total&lt;int&gt;</code>, ordinary conversions initialize the <code>int</code> parameters. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.arg.explicit#7">[temp.arg.explicit]/7</a> Supplying <code>T</code> explicitly still leaves the length to be deduced.

<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed calls with a fixed, deduced, or explicitly supplied element type. Each call deduces the pack length independently.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Element type</th><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td rowspan=2>Fixed <code>int</code></td><td><code>sum()</code></td><td>Length zero. The initialized addition fold returns zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sum(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two. Ordinary conversion initializes both <code>int</code> parameters.</td></tr>
  <tr><td rowspan=3>Deduced <code>T</code></td><td><code>total(1, 2)</code></td><td>Deduce <code>T</code> as <code>int</code> and length two.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Reject conflicting deductions for <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total()</code></td><td>Reject because there is no argument from which to deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td rowspan=2>Explicit <code>int</code></td><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two. Use the explicit element type and ordinary conversions.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;()</code></td><td>Length zero with a known element type.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The function template, its ordinary template arguments, and the pack length determine the specialization. Different counts identify different specializations. Converting arguments to a fixed element type does not add their original types to the specialization's identity. The calls <code>sum(1, 2)</code> and <code>sum(1.0, 2.0)</code> therefore select the same two-parameter specialization.

Function-type equality alone does not identify a specialization: different ordinary template arguments can produce the same adjusted function type. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.general#3">[temp.deduct.general]/3-4</a>

The programmer cannot name or explicitly supply the implicit count as a constant template parameter. For example, <code>sum&lt;3&gt;(1, 2, 3)</code> cannot specify the count. An empty template-argument list does not specify the count either so it must still be deduced. The count does not make every expression in the function body dependent.

A zero count makes no deductions for template parameters. An element type must still be supplied explicitly, deduced elsewhere, or obtained from a default. Substitution into the element pattern follows the ordinary substitution rules even when the count is zero.

Ordinary reference binding, list-initialization, parameter adjustment, and lifetime rules apply. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> A braced argument initializes its corresponding parameter and retains narrowing checks. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> A temporary bound to a reference parameter persists until completion of the full-expression containing the call. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/class.temporary#6.10">[class.temporary]/6.10</a> Declaring a pack neither extends that lifetime nor imposes a new evaluation order on argument initialization. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.call#6">[expr.call]/6-7</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Test deduction and initialization in a compiler implementation with move-only arguments, forwarding references, overloaded function names, braced arguments, constraints, exception specifications, and constant evaluation.</p>
</aside>

### Target types for initialization ### {#parameter-initialization}

A fixed parameter type supplies a destination for a braced initializer or an overloaded function name. These proposed declarations illustrate both forms:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

void plot(const Point&... points);

using Step = void (*)();
void start();
template <class T> void start(T mode);
void stop();

void run_all(Step... steps);
</xmp>

<table class=data>
<caption>Target typing under the proposed parameter-initialization rules.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>plot({0, 0}, {4, 5})</code></td><td>Each argument initializes a temporary <code>Point</code> bound to a const reference parameter.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>plot({2.5, 3})</code></td><td>Rejected because list-initialization would narrow <code>Point::x</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>run_all(start, stop)</code></td><td>The <code>Step</code> target selects <code>void start()</code> from its overload set.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

A deduced pack cannot infer a type from the bare braces or from the <code>start</code> overload set, which contains a function template. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.type#5">[temp.deduct.type]/5.8-5.9</a> Current C++ already provides those targets through <code>std::initializer_list&lt;Point&gt;</code> and <code>std::initializer_list&lt;Step&gt;</code> parameters. The corresponding calls are <code>plot({&#123;0, 0}, {4, 5}})</code> and <code>run_all({start, stop})</code>. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.list">[dcl.init.list]</a> Explicit <code>Point</code> objects and a <code>static_cast&lt;Step&gt;(start)</code> also work with deduced packs. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.over#1">[over.over]/1-3</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Test the braced-initializer calls in a compiler implementation. Compare constrained packs and initializer lists, covering deduction, empty calls and initializers, designated initializers, and narrowing. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.aggr">[dcl.init.aggr]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call">[temp.deduct.call]</a></p>
</aside>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Decide whether to support homogeneous packs in deduction guides and, if so, specify deduction and return-type rules.</p>
</aside>

### Address-taking ### {#addresses}

For the <code>sum</code> and <code>total</code> declarations above, taking an address deduces the count from the target function type. The length-two specialization of <code>sum</code> has the ordinary function type <code>int(int, int)</code>.

<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed address-taking and declaration behavior for <code>sum</code> and <code>total</code>. Each row is independent. The element type and count both need to match the identified function.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Operation</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>int (*zero)() = sum</code></td><td>Select length zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*one)(int) = sum&lt;&gt;</code><br><code>int (*two)(int, int) = sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>The two target types select different specializations of <code>sum&lt;&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;sum</code><br><code>auto two = &amp;sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>Reject because no length is determined.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;total&lt;int&gt;</code></td><td>Reject because specifying <code>T</code> does not supply the length.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(double, double) = sum</code></td><td>Reject the mismatched parameter types. Call conversions do not adapt a function pointer signature.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int...)</code> and the named definition</td><td>Corresponding declarations of the same template, if reclaiming the comma-less unnamed spelling is approved.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int, int)</code></td><td>An ordinary overload, not an implicit specialization declaration.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The existing explicit-specialization and explicit-instantiation syntax is retained. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.expl.spec#1">[temp.expl.spec]/1</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.explicit#2">[temp.explicit]/2</a> In these proposed declarations, the ordinary parameter list identifies the count:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <>
int sum<>(int value) {
    return value + 10;
}

template int sum<>(int, int);
</xmp>

Ordinary target-type matching applies after count deduction.

<aside class=issue>
<p>Specify count deduction and call-operator selection for generic-lambda conversion to a function pointer. A typed target should work, while unary plus should fail when the count is undetermined. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.closure#12">[expr.prim.lambda.closure]/12</a></p>
</aside>

The count identifies the same specialization whether the function is called or its address is taken. A runtime variadic interface would need to represent the arguments and count at runtime, without providing <code>sizeof...</code> for return types or constraints.

### Overload ordering ### {#overload-ordering}

The proposed ordering policy retains ordinary conversion ranking, partial ordering, and constraint subsumption. Homogeneity introduces no additional preference. Partial ordering compares template declarations and retains information about which parameters came from packs. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2">[over.match.best.general]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.partial#8">[temp.deduct.partial]/8</a> It does not compare only the resulting fixed-arity specializations. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.func.order#3">[temp.func.order]/3-6</a>

A call with no arguments for a pack contributes no element-type comparisons to partial ordering. A default template argument can make a candidate viable. It does not turn the candidate's dependent pattern into a fixed type for ordering. A non-template overload wins a tie against a template specialization under the existing rules. A fixed-arity template still participates in template partial ordering.

<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed overload outcomes under the ordinary-ordering policy. Each overload set is independent.</caption>
<colgroup><col><col style="width: clamp(8em, 25vw, 10em)"><col></colgroup>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Overloads</th><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td rowspan=3><code>void fixed(int... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void fixed(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>fixed(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the fixed-element template.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fixed(1.0, 2.0)</code></td><td>Select the deduced-element template by better conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fixed()</code></td><td>Select the fixed-element template because the other cannot deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td rowspan=3><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void packed(T... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class... Ts&gt; void packed(Ts... values);</code></td>
<td><code>packed(1, 2)</code></td><td>Ambiguous.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>packed(1, 2.0)</code></td><td>Select the heterogeneous template because the homogeneous candidate has conflicting deductions for <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>packed()</code></td><td>Select the heterogeneous template because the homogeneous candidate cannot deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void empty(int... values);</code><br>
<code>void empty(double... values);</code></td>
<td><code>empty()</code></td><td>Ambiguous. The zero count supplies no element-type comparison.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void pair(int, int);</code><br>
<code>void pair(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>pair(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the non-template overload after tied conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T, T);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>paired(1, 2)</code></td><td>Select the fixed-arity template under ordinary template partial ordering.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void single(T);</code><br>
<code>void single(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>single(1)</code></td><td>Ambiguous. The fixed-arity pattern and fixed element type do not establish one template as more specialized.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The implicit count is determined before evaluating an associated constraint that uses it. Constraint satisfaction uses the template arguments together with the counts of the constrained function and any enclosing homogeneous function specialization. Counts used through a concept argument or a type declared in an enclosing specialization are also inputs to constraint satisfaction.

Rechecking the same atomic constraint with the same template arguments and count bindings must give the same result. Different count bindings may give different results without changing atomic identity (existing atomic-identity rule: <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.atomic#2">[temp.constr.atomic]/2</a>). Separate predicates such as <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 1</code> and <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 2</code> gain no arithmetic implication rule. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.constr.order#1.2">[temp.constr.order]/1.2</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Specify correspondence for count expressions in atomic constraints and fold-expanded constraints. The implicit count is not a template parameter, so those rules need an explicit audit rather than an assumed mapping to an invented parameter.</p>
</aside>

### Constructors ### {#constructors}

Initializer-list constructors have priority under <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.list#1">[over.match.list]/1</a>. If homogeneous packs are permitted in constructors, those constructors would participate as implicit templates in ordinary overload selection. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/over.match.best.general#2.4">[over.match.best.general]/2.4</a>

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>A conditional constructor comparison. The two-element overload wins against the homogeneous template, while list-initialization prefers the initializer-list constructor.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>If homogeneous constructors are supported</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Values {
    Values(std::initializer_list<int>);
    explicit Values(const int&, const int&);
};
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Values {
    Values(const int&... elems);
    explicit Values(const int&, const int&);
};
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<table class=data>
<caption>Changed constructor selection under ordinary overload ranking.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Declaration</th><th scope=col>With initializer list</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous pack</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>Values values{1, 2, 3};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Selects the homogeneous constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Values values{1, 2};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Selects the explicit two-argument constructor.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Values values = {1, 2};</code></td><td>Selects the list constructor.</td><td>Rejected because the selected two-argument constructor is explicit.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Replacing the list constructor would change both the selected overload and whether copy-list-initialization succeeds.

<aside class=issue>
<p>Decide whether to permit homogeneous packs in constructors and test these examples under R2's overload model, treating the constructor as an implicit template.</p>
</aside>

## Lambdas ## {#lambdas}

The following lambda has a fixed element type and is generic solely because its pack length varies:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto product = [](double... factors) {
    return (1.0 * ... * factors);
};
</xmp>

The calls <code>product()</code> and <code>product(0.5, 0.8)</code> return <code>1.0</code> and <code>0.4</code>. A current-language lambda with <code>std::same_as&lt;double&gt; auto... factors</code> accepts both calls.

An ordinary expansion of an enclosing pack does not by itself make a lambda generic, including when the element type uses pack indexing. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.general#6">[expr.prim.lambda.general]/6</a>

## Pack indexing ## {#indexing}

A count constraint and C++26 pack indexing work the same way for a constrained pack and a homogeneous pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I>
double select_sample(double... samples)
    requires (I < sizeof...(samples)) {
    return samples...[I];
}
</xmp>

The caller supplies the index and deduction supplies the pack length. For a runtime <code>double reading</code>, <code>select_sample&lt;1&gt;(reading, 0.5, 1.0)</code> selects the second sample. Using index 3 with the same three samples fails the constraint. A <code>std::same_as&lt;double&gt; auto... samples</code> pack gives the same results for these calls. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.pack.index#2">[expr.prim.pack.index]/2-4</a>

For a type pack <code>Ts</code>, <code>Ts...[1]</code> selects its second type. When the index is itself a pack, as in <code>Ts...[Is]</code>, pack indexing expands only <code>Ts</code>. The index pack <code>Is</code> remains available to an enclosing expansion. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#6.15">[temp.variadic]/6.15</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.type.pack.index#2">[dcl.type.pack.index]/2-4</a>

The following is valid C++26. Each lambda has one unnamed parameter:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Ts, std::size_t... Is>
void indexed(std::index_sequence<Is...>) {
    (..., [](Ts...[Is]) {});
}
</xmp>

The call <code>indexed&lt;int, double, char&gt;(std::index_sequence&lt;2, 0, 2, 1&gt;{})</code> forms four lambdas with parameter types <code>char</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, and <code>double</code>. The index pack can differ in length from <code>Ts</code> and select a type more than once.

Each row below is independent and assumes enclosing declarations of <code>Ts</code> and <code>Is</code>, with valid selected indices.

<table class=data>
<caption>Indexed lambda parameters: a scalar index permits a new homogeneous count, while an index pack is expanded by the enclosing declaration or fold.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Spelling</th><th scope=col>C++26 interpretation</th><th scope=col>Proposed interpretation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]) {}</code></td><td>One unnamed parameter of the selected type.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[1]) {})</code></td><td>Ill-formed. No unexpanded pack remains for the fold.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[Is]) {})</code></td><td>The fold expands <code>Is</code> into single-parameter lambdas.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]... values) {}</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The parameter declaration has no unexpanded pack.</td><td>A homogeneous pack of the selected type with its own count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[Is]... values) {}</code></td><td>The parameter declaration expands <code>Is</code> into one parameter list.</td><td>Unchanged. No new count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[Is]... values) {})</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The inner declaration already expands <code>Is</code>.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts... values) {}</code></td><td>Ordinary expansion of the enclosing <code>Ts</code>. This alone does not make the lambda generic.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

In the varying-index form, the parameter declaration's second ellipsis already expands <code>Is</code>. This leaves no unexpanded pack for an enclosing fold. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.fold#2">[expr.prim.fold]/2</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#8">[temp.variadic]/8</a>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Confirm whether C++26 <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct#26">[dcl.fct]/26</a> treats unnamed <code>Ts...[1]...</code> as C varargs after indexing expands <code>Ts</code>. Coordinate the transition with Jan.</p>
</aside>

### Homogeneous packs of indexed types ### {#indexed-homogeneous}

A scalar index selects the proposed homogeneous pack's element type before its independent count is deduced:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I, class... Ts>
auto selected_counter() {
    return [](Ts...[I]... values) {
        return sizeof...(values);
    };
}

auto count_doubles = selected_counter<1, int, double, char>();
</xmp>

The call <code>count_doubles(0.5, 1.0)</code> returns 2. The index selects <code>double</code> from three types, but the lambda's homogeneous count comes from its two arguments. Unlike <code>Ts...[Is]... values</code>, this declaration has no index pack for its second ellipsis to expand.

### Array parameters ### {#arrays}

In C++26, the named declaration <code>int... values[Is]</code> expands <code>Is</code> into array bounds. Ordinary pointer adjustment turns parameters with positive extents 1, 2, and 3 into three <code>int*</code> parameters. Zero extents are independently invalid. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.array#1">[dcl.array]/1</a>

The C++26 <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.name#1">[dcl.name]/1</a> grammar has no array-suffix production for an abstract (unnamed) pack declarator. P2662R3 removed that production when introducing pack indexing. [[P2662R3]]

The named array-pack declaration already expands <code>Is</code>. No unexpanded occurrence of that pack remains for an enclosing fold.

The proposal retains the C++26 abstract-declarator grammar. If the committee approves reclamation, an array alias could supply an unnamed homogeneous parameter type with ordinary adjustment. Its array extent remains separate from the homogeneous count.

### Empty packs ### {#empty-and-return}

An empty outer expansion over <code>Is</code> need not instantiate an indexed selection. A fixed index must still designate an element when its type is required. Calling the proposed homogeneous lambda with zero arguments does not remove that requirement from its declaration. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.type.pack.index#2">[dcl.type.pack.index]/2</a>

## Captures ## {#captures}

Constant template parameters supply values inside a lambda without capture. This current-language function returns such a lambda:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <int... values>
auto constant_sum() {
    return [] { return (0 + ... + values); };
}
</xmp>

Calling <code>constant_sum&lt;1, 2, 3&gt;()()</code> returns <code>6</code>. The values belong to the specialization and are available whenever the returned lambda is called. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.variadic#9">[temp.variadic]/9</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.capture">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]</a>

The proposed function's parameters are <code>int</code> objects with finite lifetimes. For a runtime <code>int reading</code>, it returns a callback that stores copies of its parameter values:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto deferred_sum(int... values) {
    return [=] { return (0 + ... + values); };
}

auto sum_later = deferred_sum(reading, 2, 3);
int reading_sum = sum_later();
</xmp>

For <code>reading == 1</code>, <code>reading_sum</code> is <code>6</code> even though <code>deferred_sum</code> has returned. Replacing <code>&#91;=]</code> with <code>[]</code> makes a nonempty call ill-formed. Reference capture would leave dangling references when <code>sum_later()</code> is called. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.capture">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]</a>

A current-language function with <code>std::same_as&lt;int&gt; auto... values</code> can return the same value-capturing callback. Homogeneous packs follow the ordinary capture and lifetime rules, including capture propagation through nested lambdas. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.capture#14">[expr.prim.lambda.capture]/14</a>

## Alternatives ## {#alternatives}

This revision adopts none of these alternatives. Their examples describe each alternative's proposed behavior.

### An additional auto marker ### {#additional-auto}

The alternative spelling <code>int auto... values</code> could mark the declaration as a template with a fixed pack element type. It would require new grammar and an exception to <code>auto</code>'s ordinary role as a placeholder. A type name such as <code>int</code> is not a concept constraint. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.spec.auto.general">[dcl.spec.auto.general]</a>

Homogeneous template parameter packs already use <code>std::size_t... values</code>. Requiring <code>std::size_t auto... values</code> only for function parameters would give the two homogeneous declarations different spellings. This revision retains the unambiguous named form <code>T... values</code>. Its declarator ellipsis establishes template status.

### Empty template heads ### {#empty-template-heads}

An alternative is to allow empty template parameter lists in template declarations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK, declares a template with a homogeneous parameter pack
template <>
void foo(float... v);
</xmp>

Although this alternative is syntactically unambiguous, its resemblance to explicit specializations could confuse programmers. In an e-mail after the 2018 San Diego meeting, Richard Smith said he would oppose adopting homogeneous function parameter packs with this syntax. EWGI shared that view in the 2019 Kona poll recorded in [[#straw-polls]]. [[P1219R2]]

If empty template heads were allowed, omitting <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> from explicit specializations could make the two forms easier to distinguish. It could also make template and non-template declarations more consistent.

### Alternative specialization syntax ### {#alternative-specializations}

The <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> prefix makes an explicit specialization look like a template declaration. The function specialization shown here is an ordinary non-template entity. Its <code nohighlight>template-id</code> identifies the specialization, making the introducer redundant in this form. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.expl.spec#1">[temp.expl.spec]/1</a>

This revision retains the existing explicit-specialization syntax. The alternative could omit the <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> introducer without reducing expressivity:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// function template
template <class T, class U>
void f(T t, U u);

// ok: explicit specialization (proposed: deprecated)
template <>
void f<>(int i, double d);

// proposed: explicit specialization
void f<>(int i, double d);

// ok: ordinary function declaration (proposed: no change)
void f(int i, double d);
</xmp>

R2 assessed this alternative as follows in 2019: [[P1219R2]]

<blockquote>
<p>Under this approach, the misleading part of the syntax is removed, and the result is a more consistent language. Non-template entities no longer look like templates. For explicit specializations of function and variable templates, the old syntax can be deprecated. (The old syntax for explicit specializations of class templates probably cannot be deprecated at this time, as their use is <em>very</em> common.) Anecdotally, explicit specializations of function templates are rare and their use is generally discouraged due to subtleties around overloading.</p>
</blockquote>

EWGI in Kona in 2019 did not poll this option. R2 reports sentiment in favor of a separate paper exploring it. [[P1219R2]]

### Explicit template markers ### {#marker}

An alternative requires a marker in the template parameter list for a homogeneous pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <...>
void foo(int... v);

template <class T, ...>
void bar(T... v);
</xmp>

R2 reported implementation experience and discussions following the 2018 San Diego meeting: [[P1219R2]]

<blockquote>
<p>This idea, including the use of the ellipsis as the sigil, was suggested by Richard Smith in an impromptu conversation during the San Diego meeting. He and David Vandevoorde both indicated that this requirement would make the feature simpler to implement across a broad range of compilers. This notion is not directly supported by the experience of implementing the feature in Clang, but there is as yet no implementation experience with any other compiler. In Clang, the presence of an unparenthesized ellipsis in a declarator causes the compiler to treat the declaration as having a particular dependent type corresponding to a parameter pack, and the presence of a parameter of that type is enough to differentiate a function declaration as a template declaration instead of an explicit specialization or ordinary function declaration.</p>

<p>P1141R2, adopted in San Diego, adds support for function template declarations that lack a template head entirely; in this case, the presence of the <code>auto</code> keyword in the function parameter list indicates that the function declaration declares a function template. Any implementation that can handle these new declarations should also be able to handle homogeneous packs without added syntax. In a follow-up conversation with Richard over e-mail, he agreed with this conclusion. [[P1141R2]]</p>
</blockquote>

The corresponding lambda form would be <code>[]&lt;...&gt;(int... values) {}</code>. A marker could declare the independent count and separately opt into revised unnamed-ellipsis parsing. Making a lambda generic alone does not change indexing or move <code>Is</code> between expansions.

This revision retains abbreviated declarations without an explicit marker. A marker would make template status visible before the parameter list. Rules for count deduction, specialization identity, and overload ordering would still be needed. Adopting a marker would require separate grammar and a decision on whether unnamed-ellipsis parsing changes.

### Bare template introducer ### {#bare-template-introducer}

An alternative omits the angle brackets when no template parameters are needed:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template void foo(int... v);
</xmp>

This form resembles an explicit instantiation: both use <code>template</code> without angle brackets. An explicit instantiation does not declare a template. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.explicit#1">[temp.explicit]/1-2</a> Like an empty head, this alternative is unambiguous because the homogeneous pack identifies a template. R2 rejected it because a template declaration introduced by <code>template</code> requires an angle-bracketed parameter list. [[P1219R2]]

### Explicit lengths ### {#explicit-lengths}

An alternative allows or requires a template parameter for pack size. R2's spelling predates pack indexing: [[P1219R2]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t Len>
void foo(int...[Len] v);
</xmp>

A formal length parameter would allow callers to specify the pack length and make its role in specialization explicit. The proposed implicit count can only be deduced.

The language permits querying a pack's size with <code>sizeof...</code>, but not specifying it explicitly. Explicit lengths could apply to both homogeneous template and function parameter packs. Optional lengths would preserve the existing template-pack spelling and give both forms a consistent interface.

A type-pack variant spelled <code>Ts...[Len]</code> conflicts with C++26 indexed type selection: that spelling already selects one type from <code>Ts</code>. An explicit marker does not resolve that conflict by itself.

Constraints on <code>sizeof...</code> can restrict the implicit count. Explicit lengths, multiple homogeneous packs, and non-trailing homogeneous packs are deferred. Restoring removed abstract array-pack grammar would be a separate change.

### Trivial templates ### {#trivial-templates}

Extending the empty-template-head alternative beyond homogeneous packs would also permit templates with no template parameters and no homogeneous pack.

For homogeneous packs with non-dependent types, the empty-template-head alternative makes the <code nohighlight>template-parameter-list</code> optional in a <code nohighlight>template-head</code>. A declaration without a homogeneous pack would still require a template parameter.

Removing that requirement would allow trivial templates with no template arguments or pack count to distinguish specializations. These would differ little from non-template entities. A trivial function template could, however, be defined in a header without <code>inline</code>.

This alternative also creates an ambiguity with explicit specializations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// #1: trivial function template
template <>
void f(int v);

// #2: function template with homogeneous pack
template <>
void f(int... vs);

// #3: redeclaration of #1 or explicit specialization of #2?
template <>
void f(int v);
</xmp>

This alternative offers minor simplifications in the language specification and makes <code>[]&lt;&gt;(){}</code> well-formed. It offers little else and introduces the specialization ambiguity above.

# Reclaiming the deprecated comma-less spelling # {#compatibility}

We ask the committee to build on P3176R1 and reclaim the deprecated comma-less spelling. This would let homogeneous packs use existing pack syntax with optional parameter names. Jan Schultke's [[P3176R1]], <em>The Oxford variadic comma</em>, continued P1219R2's work on comma separation. It deprecated the comma-less spelling but retained its meaning and standalone ellipsis parameters. [[P1219R2]] <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a>

If the committee approves reclamation, <code>void send(int... values)</code> and <code>void send(int...)</code> would declare the same function template. C varargs would retain the distinct spelling <code>void send(int, ...)</code>.

Requiring pack names would avoid reclamation but add otherwise unnecessary names to declarations. An explicit marker ([[#marker]]) could preserve optional names at the cost of extra syntax. A different spelling could avoid reuse but would depart from existing pack syntax.

## Current rules and history ## {#current-comma-rules}

The February 2019 Kona EWGI poll in [[#straw-polls]] supported making the varargs comma mandatory. [[P1219-EWG-RECORD]]

A <em>varargs ellipsis</em> is a C-style ellipsis rather than a parameter-pack ellipsis. C requires an intervening comma when a parameter list precedes it. C23 also permits an ellipsis alone: [[N3096]]

<pre nohighlight>
<code nohighlight>parameter-type-list:</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-list</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-list , ...</code>
    <code nohighlight>...</code>
</pre>

C++26 also permits a standalone varargs ellipsis. Unlike C, it permits a varargs ellipsis after a parameter list without a comma. The comma-less alternative is deprecated in C++26: <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/depr.ellipsis.comma">[depr.ellipsis.comma]</a>

<pre nohighlight>
<code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-clause:</code>
    <code nohighlight>...</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list</code><sub>opt</sub>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list , ...</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list ...</code>
</pre>

C++ introduced varargs ellipses with function prototypes and initially prohibited a comma before the ellipsis. C later adopted both features but required a comma to distinguish the last formal parameter from varargs. C++ then made the comma optional for C compatibility. [[P1219R2]]

When an ellipsis at the end of a function parameter list could be part of an abstract (nameless) declarator, the parameter's type determines its meaning. If the type names an unexpanded template parameter pack or contains <code>auto</code>, the ellipsis declares a pack. Otherwise, it is a varargs ellipsis. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct#26">[dcl.fct]/26</a>

For example, these declarations have different meanings in C++26:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// declares a variadic function template with a function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T...);

// same as void f(T, ...)
template <class T>
void f(T...);
</xmp>

## Proposed change ## {#proposed-comma-change}

Under the proposal, the second declaration would declare a homogeneous function parameter pack. The proposal would also require a comma between a parameter list and a varargs ellipsis. That requirement would make the disambiguation rule unnecessary while preserving C-compatible declarations.

Reusing the spelling would break source and link compatibility under SD-10. [[SD10]] The current declaration <code>void send(int...)</code> takes one <code>int</code> followed by C varargs. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct#26">[dcl.fct]/26</a> Its proposed homogeneous meaning would change accepted calls, initialization, function types, address-taking, and template linkage consequences. The explicit comma spelling <code>void send(int, ...)</code> retains its meaning.

R2 made the following migration argument in 2019: [[P1219R2]]

<blockquote>
<p>This is a breaking change, but likely not a very impactful one. In the parlance of P0684R2, this would be a "Very Good Change": Compilers can issue warnings in C++20 mode (or earlier) whenever the disambiguation rule is resolved in favor of a varargs ellipsis, and the warning can be resolved without any change in meaning by the addition of a single character. Moreover, the number of instances should be vanishingly small. In modern C++ code, the varargs ellipsis has largely been superseded by function parameter packs. Today, apart from SFINAE uses where the disambiguation rule doesn't apply, the varargs ellipsis can mainly be found in header files that are intended to be consumed by both C and C++ code, in order to facilitate interoperability between the two languages. In these cases, the declarations must conform to the rules imposed by C syntax, and so they will already conform to the rules proposed here. Lastly, personal experience suggests that the vast majority of C++ users aren't even aware that the comma preceding a varargs ellipsis is optional. [[P0684R2]]</p>
</blockquote>

[[P3776R1]], <em>More trailing commas</em>, proposes trailing commas in function parameter lists but excludes them after C ellipsis parameters. Its proposed grammar preserves the distinction between function parameter packs and C ellipsis parameters.

<aside class=issue>
<p>Agree with Jan on language modes, migration, and trailing-comma wording. Decide whether to remove the old meaning before or alongside homogeneous packs, or use different syntax. Check shared C/C++ headers, macros, and address-taking code. If reclamation is declined, specify a consistent explicit alternative. [[P3776R1]]</p>
</aside>

Reclamation would also require migrating the named comma-less form <code>void send(int value...)</code> to <code>void send(int value, ...)</code>. The name cannot be moved across the ellipsis as an implicit reinterpretation.

## The impact of a breaking change ## {#compatibility-survey}

The survey and Boost analysis below come from P1219R2 (2019) and do not survey current code. R2 describes the ACTCD19 dataset at codesearch.isocpp.org (2019 archive). It reports 876,800,403 lines of source code across 2,489,599 files from open-source projects, citing the codesearch FAQ. The survey used this dataset to estimate the impact of requiring a comma before a varargs ellipsis. [[P1219R2]] [[CODESEARCH-FAQ]]

A C++ parser with sufficient language support and build scripts for each project were unavailable. The survey therefore used regular expression searches and human review.

The survey considered only headers with extensions <code>.h</code>, <code>.hh</code>, <code>.hpp</code>, or <code>.hxx</code>. It stripped preprocessor directives, then ran each file through the C++ preprocessor to remove comments. Because the macro definitions had been removed, invocations remained unexpanded. Inactive blocks, including <code>#if 0</code>, also remained. Scanning the output for ellipses yielded 258,196 lines, each tagged with its source file.

Lines with identifiers, operators, or other patterns characteristic of pack declarations or expansions were excluded from further analysis.

In 89,614 lines, a comma immediately preceded an ellipsis. These lines unambiguously declared varargs functions.

Of 8,101 lines beginning with an ellipsis, 6,241 appeared to come from function declarations. Of those 6,241 lines, 6,092 had a comma immediately before the ellipsis on a preceding line. Of the remaining 149 lines, 131 were catch clauses or declarations with only an ellipsis. The last 18 mixed static-analysis macros into parameter lists. None of these ellipses is ambiguous with a pack declaration.

<table class="data numeric-table">
<caption>Comma-less varargs survey reported in P1219R2, 2019. [[P1219R2]]</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Classification</th><th scope=col>Lines</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Lines containing ellipses</td><td>258,196</td></tr>
<tr><td>Varargs ellipses, with comma</td><td>95,724</td></tr>
<tr><td>Varargs ellipses, without comma</td><td>6,318</td></tr>
<tr><td>From Boost</td><td>6,182</td></tr>
<tr><td>From Boost, deduplicated</td><td>879</td></tr>
<tr><td>All others</td><td>136</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The dataset contained seventeen complete or partial copies of different Boost versions.

Of the 879 unique Boost hits, 416 came from <code>type_traits/detail/is_mem_fun_pointer_tester.hpp</code>. The <code>is_mem_fun_pointer_tester</code> overloads in that file recognize member-function-pointer types with arities from 0 to 24. They cover every combination of qualifiers and calling convention, with and without varargs. Variadic templates and class template partial specialization can reduce the 2,759-line file to a handful of lines. Most other Boost hits follow a similar pattern that supports generic programming in pre-C++11 modes.

<aside class=issue>
<p>Update the code-search survey and Boost analysis using recent data, documenting the dataset and methodology.</p>
</aside>

